    "result_text": "All 284 tests pass with a clean build. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on owner creation. When an owner is created, the response now includes `locality`: `\"local\"` if their city is the **single most common** city among existing owners at that moment, otherwise `\"remote\"` (a tie for most-common yields `\"remote\"`, since there's no single most common city).\n\nChanges made, following the project's existing OfficeFloor patterns (mirroring the sibling `namesakeCount` / `sharesHousehold` rules):\n\n1. **`AssignLocality.java`** (new function) \u2014 tallies existing owners' cities via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, finds the strict unique maximum, and sets `local`/`remote`. Runs before the owner is saved, so the new owner is excluded from the tally, and after `NormalizeCity` so it compares canonical city spellings.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignLocality` into the pipeline (after `assignSharesHousehold`, before `save`), under the transaction governance.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `locality` field with getter/setter and a `@Column(name = \"locality\")` mapping.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema (regenerates `OwnerDto`; MapStruct auto-maps it by name, so it's returned on both POST and GET).\n\n5. **Schema SQL** (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `locality` column so the value persists and is returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}` (which the acceptance test reads back).\n\nThe new `Cp18Tests` passes, and the full suite (284 tests) is green.",
